# FAQs
faqs:
  - question: How many downspouts does my home need?
    answer: "<p>One for every 25-30 feet of gutter, minimum. A 50-foot run needs at least two. Lots of older Albertville homes have one downspout on a 40-foot run and wonder why it overflows - the math just doesn't work. Adding a downspout or two often solves chronic overflow problems.</p>"
  - question: My downspouts dump water right by the foundation. Is that bad?
    answer: "<p>Yes. Water needs to exit at least 4 feet from the foundation - 6 feet is better, 10 is best. Every inch closer than that increases foundation risk. On Sand Mountain's clay soil, water doesn't soak in; it pools and finds its way into cracks.</p>"
  - question: What size downspouts are best?
    answer: "<p>Standard 2x3-inch downspouts handle 600 square feet of roof each. 3x4-inch handle 1,200 - double the capacity for maybe $15 more per downspout. Given how hard it rains here (55+ inches annually, often 2-3 inches in an hour), 3x4 is almost always worth it.</p>"
  - question: Can you add downspouts to my existing gutters?
    answer: "<p>Usually, yes. If gutters are in decent shape, we cut a new outlet, install the downspout, and extend it away from the house. Often fixes overflow problems for $150-250 per location.</p>"
  - question: Why do my downspouts keep clogging?
    answer: "<p>Debris getting past your gutters. If it's happening regularly, you either need gutter guards or strainers at the outlets. We can also upsize to 3x4 - larger openings clog less easily.</p>"

