{% set local_problem %}
<p>A lot of Albertville homes have downspout problems that owners don't even realize. We looked at a house on Highway 431 last month where the homeowner called about "gutters overflowing." The gutters were fine - properly sized, clean, good slope. The problem? Two 2x3 downspouts trying to drain a 1,400-square-foot roof section. They'd overflow every time rain exceeded about half an inch per hour.</p>

<p>We added a third downspout and upsized all three to 3x4. Cost: $425. Problem solved permanently.</p>

<p>Common downspout problems we see in Albertville:</p>

<p><strong>Not enough downspouts.</strong> Builders minimize them to save cost. One downspout every 40 feet isn't enough for heavy rain.</p>

<p><strong>Undersized.</strong> 2x3 downspouts on large roof sections that need 3x4.</p>

<p><strong>Dumping at foundation.</strong> Extensions that broke off, were removed for mowing, or were never installed.</p>

<p><strong>Disconnected.</strong> Bottom elbow fell off, water splashes against the house.</p>

<p>On Sand Mountain's clay-and-loam soil, water doesn't soak in quickly. When downspouts deposit water next to your house, it pools, and eventually finds its way into your basement or crawlspace. The fix is usually simple and affordable - but waiting until you have foundation cracks is not.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three parts: enough capacity, good positioning, and proper extensions.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity</strong> means having enough downspouts, big enough to handle your roof's water volume. Standard math: one 3x4 downspout handles 1,200 square feet of roof area, or 40 linear feet of gutter. If you have more roof than that draining to one downspout, you'll overflow during heavy rain.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning</strong> means putting downspouts where water naturally collects - at low points in the gutter run, at corners, and near valleys where roof sections concentrate flow.</p>

<p><strong>Extensions</strong> carry water away from the foundation. Minimum 4 feet; 6-10 feet is better. For permanent solutions, underground drains route water even further.</p>
{% endset %}

